# Bytecode caches. A `git add -A` of this directory committed four of these
# once; they are build output and must never be tracked. Kept here rather than
# in the repository root so it cannot conflict with upstream's own .gitignore.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
# The checker's LEAN_PATH, captured per container, and the scratch files the
# tactic battery and the refutation prober write.
leanpath.txt
.refute.*
# check.sh's flock files, so at most two checks run at once. Runtime state, and
# committing a lock file would be meaningless; check.sh recreates the directory.
logs/
# THE LEASE AND STEP STATE. Untracked, and that was the whole problem: a worker
# clearing the tree with `git clean -fd` deletes untracked directories but
# SPARES ignored ones, so this list is what stands between a tidy-up and the
# loss of every live lease id. It has already happened once - the ignored
# neighbours above (leanpath.txt, logs/, __pycache__) survived while live/,
# dead/, steps/ and done/ went, which is exactly that signature. We lost
# L-meta-9754 (1189 points) and L-meta-1988 to it, and then kept leasing steps
# we silently dropped because desk.sh could not write into a missing steps/.
#
# Never commit them: a lease id is a credential with a 20-minute life and the
# step JSON is the desk's copy, refetchable with `desk.sh state`.
live/
dead/
steps/
done/
# The capacity the desk last reported ("You hold 2 of 2 steps. 2 grows as your
# proofs are checked."), which poll.sh reads instead of guessing. Same class of
# runtime state, same reason to keep it out of a commit and out of reach of a
# tidy-up.
capacity
capacity_note
